   "md": "# Vault Security Review\n\nGenerated: 2026-10-17T07:30:00.000Z\nChain: ethereum\nSchema: srp.audit/v1\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nSRP reviewed 1 source file(s) and produced 3 evidence-backed finding(s), 3 proven by an executed test.\nFindings are static analysis results. A finding is proven only when a locally executed PoC or test result is attached. Unproven high and critical findings are shown at medium with their proposed severity.\n\n## Coverage\n\nAnalyzed 1 of 1 file(s) (100%), 5 function(s) or instruction(s) indexed. Analyzers: srp-pattern, srp-structural.\nNo finding is not the same as no bug. See residual unknowns below.\n\n## Findings\n\n### Authorization relies on tx.origin\n\nSeverity: high\nConfidence: verified (0.95)\nStatus: proven\nDetector: evm-tx-origin-auth\n\nImpact: A malicious contract called by the owner can pass the check and run privileged actions in the owner's name.\n\nAttack path: Attacker deploys a contract the owner will call -> Owner calls it -> Contract calls the protocol while tx.origin is the owner -> Privileged action runs for the attacker\n\nExploitability: Proven by a locally executed test: phishing contract called by the owner swept the vault.\n\nRemediation: Authorize on msg.sender. Use tx.origin only to block contracts, never to grant rights.\n\nEvidence:\n- Vault.sol:29-29 `require(tx.origin == owner, \"not owner\");` (observed, srp-pattern): tx.origin is the original EOA, not the direct caller, so any contract the owner interacts with can act as the owner.\n\nDebate:\n- Attacker: Reachability: the pattern at Vault.sol:L29 `require(tx.origin == owner, \"not owner\");` sits in code an attacker can trigger. Defender: Reachability is not established structurally; the pattern match alone does not show an entrypoint. Judge: Plausible but unproven. Needs a harness run.\n- Attacker: State effect: A malicious contract called by the owner can pass the check and run privileged actions in the owner's name. Defender: Only one line is cited. The state change the attack needs is not shown. Judge: Plausible but unproven. Needs a harness run.\n- Attacker: Requests severity as proposed. Defender: High severity needs an executed PoC; until then it stays capped. Judge: Plausible but unproven. Needs a harness run.\n\nValidation: proven (executed). Run: `forge test --match-test test_txorigin_phish -vvv`\n\n### Reentrancy: withdraw writes balances after an external call\n\nSeverity: high\nConfidence: verified (0.95)\nStatus: proven\nDetector: evm-reentrancy-external-call\n\nImpact: The callee can re-enter before state is updated and repeat the flow against stale balances or accounting.\n\nAttack path: Attacker deploys a contract whose receive/fallback or token hook calls back -> Attacker calls withdraw -> At L17 control passes to the attacker contract before balances is updated -> Attacker re-enters withdraw and the check passes again against the stale balances\n\nExploitability: Proven by a locally executed test: attacker drained 10 ETH with 1 ETH deposited.\n\nRemediation: Move the write to balances before the external call (checks-effects-interactions) and add nonReentrant to value-moving entrypoints.\n\nEvidence:\n- Vault.sol:17-17 `(bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: balances[msg.sender]}(\"\");` (observed, srp-structural): External call transfers control before state is final.\n- Vault.sol:19-19 `balances[msg.sender] = 0;` (derived, srp-structural): State write to balances happens after the external call.\n\nFacts:\n- withdraw is external, callable by anyone (L16)\n- external call at L17 hands control to the callee\n- state variable balances is written at L19, after the call\n- withdraw has no reentrancy guard modifier\n\nDebate:\n- Attacker: Reachability: withdraw is external, callable by anyone (L16). Defender: Accepted: withdraw is external. Judge: Credible. Proceed to validation.\n- Attacker: State effect: Vault.sol:L19 `balances[msg.sender] = 0;` follows Vault.sol:L17 `(bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: balances[msg.sender]}(\"\");`, which is the unsafe ordering. Defender: Cannot dispute the ordering from source; both lines are cited. Judge: Credible. Proceed to validation.\n- Attacker: Requests severity as proposed. Defender: High severity needs an executed PoC; until then it stays capped. Judge: Credible. Proceed to validation.\n\nValidation: proven (executed). Run: `forge test --match-contract SRP_evm_reentrancy_external_call_withdraw -vvv`\n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n// Generated by SRP for Vault.sol. Fill in the target constructor/deposit, then: forge test --match-contract SRP_evm_reentrancy_external_call_withdraw -vvv\npragma solidity ^0.8.20;\nimport \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\n\ninterface ITarget { function withdraw() external; }\n\ncontract Reenter {\n    ITarget public target; uint256 public depth;\n    constructor(ITarget t) { target = t; }\n    function attack() external { target.withdraw(); }\n    receive() external payable { if (depth++ < 5 && address(target).balance > 0) target.withdraw(); }\n}\n\ncontract SRP_evm_reentrancy_external_call_withdraw is Test {\n    function test_reenter_withdraw() public {\n        // ITarget target = ITarget(address(new Target()));  // deploy and fund the target here\n        // Reenter attacker = new Reenter(target);           // give the attacker a legitimate deposit\n        // uint256 before = address(attacker).balance;\n        // attacker.attack();\n        // assertGt(address(attacker).balance - before, depositAmount, \"re-entry drained more than the deposit\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Missing access control: setOwner writes privileged state owner\n\nSeverity: high\nConfidence: verified (0.95)\nStatus: proven\nDetector: evm-missing-access-control\n\nImpact: Any account can call it and take over ownership, configuration, or funds.\n\nAttack path: Attacker finds the unguarded function -> Attacker calls setOwner -> Contract writes privileged state owner -> Attacker controls the privileged role or asset\n\nExploitability: Proven by a locally executed test: non-owner became owner and swept the vault.\n\nRemediation: Restrict setOwner with onlyOwner / AccessControl, or an initializer modifier if it is a one-time setup function.\n\nEvidence:\n- Vault.sol:23-23 `function setOwner(address newOwner) external {` (observed, srp-structural): setOwner is external with no modifiers.\n- Vault.sol:24-24 `owner = newOwner;` (derived, srp-structural): setOwner writes privileged state owner without checking msg.sender.\n\nFacts:\n- setOwner is external with no modifiers (L23)\n- body never compares msg.sender\n- L24: writes privileged state owner\n\nDebate:\n- Attacker: Reachability: setOwner is external with no modifiers (L23). Defender: Accepted: setOwner is external. Judge: Credible. Proceed to validation.\n- Attacker: State effect: Vault.sol:L24 `owner = newOwner;` follows Vault.sol:L23 `function setOwner(address newOwner) external {`, which is the unsafe ordering. Defender: Cannot dispute the ordering from source; both lines are cited. Judge: Credible. Proceed to validation.\n- Attacker: Requests severity as proposed. Defender: High severity needs an executed PoC; until then it stays capped. Judge: Credible. Proceed to validation.\n\nValidation: proven (executed). Run: `forge test --match-contract SRP_evm_missing_access_control_setOwner -vvv`\n\n```solidity\n// Generated by SRP for Vault.sol: forge test --match-contract SRP_evm_missing_access_control_setOwner -vvv\npragma solidity ^0.8.20;\nimport \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\ncontract SRP_evm_missing_access_control_setOwner is Test {\n    function test_anyone_can_call_setOwner() public {\n        address attacker = makeAddr(\"attacker\");\n        vm.prank(attacker);\n        // target.setOwner(...);  // expect success, then assert the privileged state now belongs to attacker\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Residual Unknowns\n\n- None recorded.\n\n## Protocol Intent\n\n- Only the owner may sweep funds or change ownership.\n- The vault must remain solvent after withdrawals.\n\n## Threat Model\n\n- Abuse asset withdrawal and transfer flow to violate protocol guarantees",
